{"causes": ["The rise of modernism in the arts set the stage for minimalist music as a reaction against the complexity of previous musical forms.", "Technological advancements in recording and amplification allowed for new ways to present and appreciate music, influencing minimalist practices.", "Cultural shifts in the 1960s, including the counterculture movement, encouraged experimental approaches to art and music, fostering the growth of minimalist music."], "effects": ["Minimalist music significantly influenced contemporary classical music, leading to the emergence of new genres and styles that prioritize simplicity and repetition.", "The principles of minimalist music have permeated various artistic fields, including visual arts and dance, encouraging interdisciplinary collaborations and innovative performances.", "The global reach of minimalist music has inspired diverse musical traditions, resulting in a fusion of genres and the birth of new soundscapes in world music."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "avant-garde-music", "sourceName": "Avant-Garde Music", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "minimalist-music", "targetName": "Minimalist Music", "context": "Avant-garde music in the mid-20th century laid the groundwork for the emergence of minimalist music, particularly through experimental practices."}, {"sourceSlug": "1960s-counterculture", "sourceName": "1960s Counterculture", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "minimalist-music", "targetName": "Minimalist Music", "context": "The 1960s counterculture movement encouraged a reevaluation of artistic norms, directly contributing to the development of minimalist music."}, {"sourceSlug": "new-york-city", "sourceName": "New York City", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "minimalist-music", "targetName": "Minimalist Music", "context": "Many key performances and developments in minimalist music occurred in New York City, particularly during the 1960s and 1970s."}, {"sourceSlug": "steve-reich", "sourceName": "Steve Reich", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "minimalist-music", "targetName": "Minimalist Music", "context": "Steve Reich's innovative compositions helped define and shape the minimalist music movement, influencing countless artists."}, {"sourceSlug": "philip-glass", "sourceName": "Philip Glass", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "minimalist-music", "targetName": "Minimalist Music", "context": "Philip Glass's work within the minimalist music genre has established him as one of its most significant figures, collaborating with various artists and genres."}], "places": [{"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "San Francisco, USA", "role": "Influential Scene"}, {"name": "Berlin, Germany", "role": "Key Venue"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: minimalist music", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "minimalist-music"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q572901", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minimal_music"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "minimalist music contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Minimalist music reshaped contemporary music by introducing new compositional techniques and philosophies, influencing a generation of artists across various genres. Its focus on simplicity and repetition has had lasting effects on music, art, and culture globally.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-07T17:22:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28885137047", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Form of art music or other compositional practice that employs limited or minimal musical materials Created c. 1964.", "newValue": "Minimalist music emerged in the mid-20th century as a revolutionary art form characterized by the use of limited musical materials, notable for its significant impact on contemporary music and culture. This movement began to take shape around 1964, primarily in the United States, with prominent figures such as Steve Reich, Philip Glass, and Terry Riley leading the charge. Minimalist music is defined by its repetitive structures, gradual transformations, and a focus on the essence of sound itself, challenging the conventions of traditional Western music and paving the way for new artistic expressions.

The foundational works of minimalist composers were often marked by landmark performances, such as Riley's "In C" (1964), which encouraged improvisation and collaboration among musicians. Steve Reich's compositions, such as "Music for 18 Musicians" (1976), exemplified the use of phasing techniques, where two identical melodies drift apart and converge, creating complex textures from simple ideas. This period also saw the rise of venues like the Kitchen in New York, which became a hub for avant-garde music and performance arts, fostering an environment where minimalist music could thrive and evolve.

The legacy of minimalist music is profound, influencing a wide range of genres, including electronic, ambient, and even pop music, as artists like Brian Eno and the band Radiohead integrated minimalist principles into their work. Minimalism's emphasis on process and listener perception has led to a reevaluation of what constitutes music, encouraging audiences to find beauty in simplicity and repetition. The movement has inspired countless artists worldwide and remains a crucial touchstone for ongoing explorations in sound and composition."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-07T17:22:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28885137047", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Cultural, artistic, or intellectual creation"], "newValue": ["The rise of modernism in the arts set the stage for minimalist music as a reaction against the complexity of previous musical forms.", "Technological advancements in recording and amplification allowed for new ways to present and appreciate music, influencing minimalist practices.", "Cultural shifts in the 1960s, including the counterculture movement, encouraged experimental approaches to art and music, fostering the growth of minimalist music."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-07T17:22:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28885137047", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": ["Minimalist music significantly influenced contemporary classical music, leading to the emergence of new genres and styles that prioritize simplicity and repetition.", "The principles of minimalist music have permeated various artistic fields, including visual arts and dance, encouraging interdisciplinary collaborations and innovative performances.", "The global reach of minimalist music has inspired diverse musical traditions, resulting in a fusion of genres and the birth of new soundscapes in world music."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-07T17:22:55Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#288851370